#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505883} :activity/id #uuid "895899f5-e0e0-50f3-bd94-fa13c6acacef" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048441 :topic/id #uuid "9faf7c3f-8d4f-5e48-97ed-6c10e2c5383a" :topic/math-academy-id 291} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505862 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d86fb7c-04a2-5054-88db-d87318f91df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245716} {:answer/id #uuid "a7efd507-c58e-56ec-8841-05d7624ba7c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245816} {:answer/id #uuid "b538f1e2-5740-5e86-9585-792990790c3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245930} {:answer/id #uuid "cf18104f-33a0-52e9-88d9-69f208eb80ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246163} {:answer/id #uuid "f4626bee-994c-50c4-b9f0-fb3dfa085a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246468}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246468} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b79bcdf-534b-5594-b18b-23bc8c71894d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "697e17f6-464a-5495-90d8-1dc0fdda0186" :question/math-academy-id "q-110338" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+10}{x+1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(4).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(4)=x.$ For $x\\ne -1,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(4) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =4 \\\\ \\frac{x+10}{x+1} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{x+10}{x+1}\\cdot (x+1) & =4\\cdot (x+1) \\\\ \\frac{x+10}{\\cancel{(x+1)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(x+1)} & =4x+4 \\\\ x+10 & =4x+4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(4)=2.$\n\nNow we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+10}{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{9}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-\\frac{9}{{(2+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{{(3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{9} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "455e56d5-f225-535e-825f-94cbf70b48fc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505880}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505880 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505744 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fd15925-6269-57a7-be2d-c5bb4a253804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505158}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505158} :answer-field/id #uuid "3719cd77-cf8e-5efe-870f-d9b67a741f79" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1fb55561-256d-590b-a263-00ef7bf01b04" :question/math-academy-id "q-342578" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=8x-{x}^{4}$ and the point $P(1,7)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(7,1)$ is$\\,${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(1,7).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =8x-{x}^{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =8-4{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(1,7)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(7,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(7) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(7))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "975b5dbf-d467-5d52-8a17-1f76882fba02" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505885}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505883 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247103 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0de97e9b-167d-58b8-b3d5-94d7072f96fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244438} {:answer/id #uuid "411a9d60-45d9-5255-af7c-f54d7ac9a709" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244886} {:answer/id #uuid "7480aff9-21a8-5cfa-9c98-bc986f215b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245351} {:answer/id #uuid "a3928f90-c565-5339-83c1-14d12d515196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{44}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245773} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d9aa97-30ab-5be0-9392-8e2eebc6259c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246375}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245351} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f27b98d-efdc-5fef-8552-5c0790435d8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "32e90e8e-86b1-556f-b1e0-fc9bc969a8b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-110284" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(3)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $-3$ |\n| $-2$ | $3$ | $-5$ | $-4$ | $2$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function ${f}^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(x)=\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}+{g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(3)=\\frac{g(3)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}+{g}^{′}(3){f}^{-1}(3).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(3)=-2,$ $g(3)=-1,$ ${g}^{′}(3)=-3,$ and ${f}^{′}(-2)=-5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(3) & =\\frac{g(3)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}+{g}^{′}(3){f}^{-1}(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{f}^{′}(-2)}+(-3)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-5}+6 \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a0294a61-633a-557c-873e-f9769cf7d36d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505862}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505885 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505765 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff4aa7f4-8cb5-5cc7-8654-d178bd47988c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505603}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505603} :answer-field/id #uuid "be729943-ac78-5a58-a4af-33ce85ae0d28" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505700}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "513e391c-326d-528e-9c7c-38c97cf324b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-342598" :question/problem "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $5$ | $3$ | $-2$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(4)={g}^{′}(4){f}^{-1}(4)+\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(4)=2,$ $g(4)=-1,$ ${g}^{′}(4)=\\frac{1}{2},$ and ${f}^{′}(2)=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(4) & ={g}^{′}(4){f}^{-1}(4)+\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2+\\frac{(-1)}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{(-1)}{5} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "acef38f7-9530-5971-b272-29c746a33a1a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505891}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505891 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505782 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "521f1d1c-c4a9-54ce-be0a-26a5043158cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505268}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505268} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dc0e4ad-a39d-502a-8eb5-af62880a7035" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "70bc61eb-58b7-5349-9bef-f900f312799d" :question/math-academy-id "q-342615" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =2 \\\\ x & ={2}^{2} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=4.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(4),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d3f19cde-6964-5e90-b86b-fab6f1784841"}] :activity/title "Differentiating an Inverse Function at a Point" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505099}]}
